WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.060
In 2018, the North American box office has
had its share of high-profile bombs.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:09.780
There was Solo, the worst-performing Star
Wars movie in the history of Star Wars, and

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.100
Skyscraper, the millionth movie Dwayne Johnson's
made this year.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:17.740
But nothing even comes close to the disastrous
performance of the Chinese film Asura.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:24.900
Made with a reported budget of $113 million,
Asura debuted to just $7.1 million, and before

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.619
the weekend was over, it had been pulled from
theaters.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:32.680
So, why did China's most expensive movie ever
made bomb so hard at the box office?

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.520
What is Asura?

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:39.730
An adaptation of ancient Tibetan legends and
Buddhist mythology, Asura follows a young

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:43.660
man who discovers he's destined to save a
heavenly dimension, which is under attack

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:45.780
by a three-headed king named Asura.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:50.180
Over the course of his journey, he discovers
he's the reincarnation of one of Asura's heads,

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:53.280
which he'd lost 100 years ago during a previous
invasion attempt.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.631
Asura is about a hero's journey, one that
was pitched as the start of a trilogy, but

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.060
it doesn't look like any sequels are coming
anytime soon.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:01.960
Sabotage

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:07.400
Asura's producers are accusing China's most
prominent film review aggregating websites

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.800
of letting users tank the movie's rating with
illegitimate reviews.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:15.420
There are three sites in China that work as
the equivalent of Rotten Tomatoes and Metacritic.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:20.320
Asura's early average score on one was an
8.4 out of 10, while the other two registered

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.380
at 4.9 and a 3.1.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.240
What explains that discrepancy?

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.329
The people behind Asura are convinced that
their movie is the victim of a focused, malicious

00:01:28.329 --> 00:01:28.829
attack.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.100
Specifically, the producers claim that they
discovered a large number of 1 out of 10 scores

00:01:33.109 --> 00:01:37.770
from suspicious-looking users which were posted
immediately after the film hit theaters.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:39.689
In an official statement, they have announced,

00:01:39.689 --> 00:01:42.960
"Whoever is behind this is dirty, stupid and
ridiculous."

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.240
Yanked from theaters

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:50.100
Rather than cut their losses and ride out
the storm, Asura's filmmakers decided to snatch

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.800
their movie back out of theaters as quickly
as possible.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.400
Only a few other movies have gotten the hook
in China.

00:01:55.409 --> 00:02:00.759
In 2012, Quentin Tarantino's Django Unchained
was unexpectedly pulled from Chinese theaters

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:02.619
literally minutes after being released.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.780
But that was only done for the sake of censorship,
and Django returned to theaters in a matter

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:08.420
of weeks after edits were made.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:10.660
Asura's situation is entirely different.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.780
It was pulled by the film's own producers,
for the drastic purposes of editing it to

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:15.960
somehow make it better.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:20.020
But to appreciably raise the movie's quality
through editing would likely take months,

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:20.800
as opposed to weeks.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:23.160
World-class Effects

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:27.860
One of Asura's biggest attempted innovations
is its larger-than-usual focus on special

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:28.360
effects.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:32.819
This might seem unusual to American moviegoers,
who are used to seeing state-of-the-art special

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:34.510
effects as a matter of course.

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:38.760
But in the past, Chinese-produced movies typically
haven't had as much money pumped into them.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:42.340
As a result, their special effects often looked
embarrassingly cheap.

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:44.200
Asura aimed to break that mold.

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:48.489
It seems no expense was spared to bring the
movie's effects to the standard set by American

00:02:48.489 --> 00:02:48.980
movies.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:53.640
That included hiring in-demand, award-winning
Hollywood talent, including veterans of Deadpool,

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:55.690
Lord of the Rings, and Furious 7.

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:59.150
But while the money seems to have all ended
up on screen, it doesn't appear to have been

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:00.040
a good investment.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:02.180
Middling marketing

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:06.780
Another one of Asura's problems is the sort
of thing that could strike any movie, anywhere:

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:08.500
a lack of effective marketing.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:12.959
Despite six years' worth of development and
build up, when it came time to actually sell

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:16.909
Asura to potential audiences, it looks like
the ball got dropped at a critical moment.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:21.579
At least, that's one claim that's been circulating,
stemming from Chinese market analysts.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:25.750
According to a representative of Beijing-based
showbiz market research firm Fankink,

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:30.500
"Based on our tracking, prerelease market
heat for this movie was quite low, below average."

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:34.959
Regardless of whether or not that's an accurate
assessment of the situation, Asura now has

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.760
a reputation as a movie almost nobody wanted
to see.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:39.840
Untested director

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:41.820
What else could have gone wrong here?

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:45.100
For one thing, director Peng Zhang is an untested
quantity.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:49.220
Though he's been a stunt coordinator for many
Hollywood productions, including Wonder Woman,

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:53.740
The Last Samurai, and Rush Hour 3, Asura is
his first ever project as director.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:57.940
Think about that for a second: What's purported
to be the most expensive film ever made in

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:02.060
the history of China was directed by a person
who had never directed a movie before in his

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:02.560
life.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:04.719
We're not saying Asura's failure is Zhang's
fault.

00:04:04.719 --> 00:04:08.400
We're just pointing out that a first-time
director seems like an incredibly strange

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:11.660
choice to head up such an expensive, high-stakes
production.

